# Korean PDP copy (모든 브랜드)

When adding or updating **any** Briq products (Prada, Gucci, Chanel, Burberry, Belstaff, Paul Smith, Arc'teryx, Galvin Green, London Undercover, Christopher Ward, etc.):

1. **Translate everything the shopper reads as body copy** into natural Korean:
   - `descriptionKo`, `featuresKo`
   - `storySections[].titleKo` / `bodyKo` (except short “갤러리” placeholders)
   - materials, care, detail bullets
   - Prefer Korean `nameKo` / `colorNameKo` when a natural fashion translation exists; short style names may stay Latin when intentional.
2. **Do not ship hybrid EN/KO** (glossary word-swap on English sentences). If the translate API fails, use curated phrase maps / copy JSON or retry — never cache leftover English as success.
3. Brand/tech tokens may stay Latin when intentional (`Re-Nylon`, `GORE-TEX`, `Chanel`, sizes, mm/cm).
4. After rebuilding a catalogue, **always verify**:
   ```bash
   python3 scripts/check-catalog-korean.py --brand <pr|gc|ch|bb|bs|ps|ax|gg|lu|cw> --fail
   ```
   For newly registered SKUs only (weekly / large syncs):
   ```bash
   python3 scripts/check-catalog-korean.py --brand <brand> --new-since <ISO-Z> --fail
   ```
   For specific updated ids:
   ```bash
   python3 scripts/check-catalog-korean.py --brand <brand> --ids id1,id2 --fail
   ```
5. Shared helpers live in `scripts/ko_qa.py`. Weekly syncs call `weekly_korean_gate.check_new_korean` after build — do not remove that gate.
6. Prefer curated Korean copy files (`*-ko-copy.json`, translate caches with polish) for long editorial text when gtx is rate-limited.
